"A Petri net models concurrent behavior through places, transitions, tokens, and explicitly defined firing rules.\n\nUse when: Reason about resource contention, synchronization, and deadlock. Explore reachable configurations of concurrent software behavior.\n\nAvoid when: Readers only need a simple narrative sequence.\n\nVisual grammar: Circles denote places and bars or rectangles denote transitions. Directed arcs connect places to transitions or transitions to places. Tokens encode the current marking; arc weights encode required token counts.\n\nBuild: Declare the net variant and initial marking. Model resource or condition places separately from transition actions. Connect input and output places and state any weights or capacities. Lay out concurrent branches in parallel with enough space to count tokens and read arc labels.\n\nCheck: Arcs preserve the place-transition bipartite structure. Enabling and firing follow the declared token rules. Deadlock or boundedness claims have analysis evidence, not visual intuition alone. Token marks and arc weights remain clear of connector intersections.\n\nPitfalls: Assuming a transition fires merely because one input place has a token. Mixing inhibitor or colored-net notation into an unexplained ordinary net.\n\nExample: Two workers compete for a single lock token; each must return it after finishing its critical section.\n\nAlternatives: Activity diagram, Transaction schedule diagram."
